<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Deutsche Welle: DW.com</title><subtitle type="html">Deutsche Welle: DW.com</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-asia</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:03:18Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-asia" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12758?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-asia" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>The billion-euro trade driving Europe's eels to extinction</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:63a01d78ddaa24d4f4d80db4d3fd33ad06cf9f4f95cdef512784b2e3c1b1fc65</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:02:45Z</updated><published>2026-09-09T08:36:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-billion-euro-trade-driving-europe-s-eels-to-extinction/a-78523145--e574ad564b10.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-billion-euro-trade-driving-europe-s-eels-to-extinction/a-78523145?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Traffickers are making big bucks smuggling millions of critically endangered eels across continents, using suitcases on planes and legal loopholes to deliver them to dinnerplates. Where are the smuggling hotspots?</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>India: 5 baby orangutans rescued, smuggling suspected</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b0a77d3a5eff7b9bc506d1af982f178960ea64e6e223e6222915e7c77871e8bd</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:02:47Z</updated><published>2026-09-09T08:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/india-5-baby-orangutans-rescued-smuggling-suspected/live-79195914--34bc38c447a6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/india-5-baby-orangutans-rescued-smuggling-suspected/live-79195914?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Five young orangutans, not native to India, were found in an Odisha forest, sparking suspicion of an international smuggling operation. Meanwhile, India said it was not involved in the BAPS-Eiffel Tower row.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Trafficked into fraud: An African crisis situation in Cambodia</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:bacfbbc8047ba53a96d798f79be5522bc4237f3356e13c84d0b1b37c42e07586</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:02:50Z</updated><published>2026-09-09T08:27:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/trafficked-into-fraud-an-african-crisis-situation-in-cambodia/a-79189967--d423991a2cca.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/trafficked-into-fraud-an-african-crisis-situation-in-cambodia/a-79189967?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Cambodia's crackdown on scam operators is stranding young African trafficking victims in the Asian country with no money, no passport and no prospects.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>How Mao Zedong's legacy still shapes China 50 years on</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:73bcb6e9545d5167a6a45c347fb0556726ed33c3ed08ec1802437087429c6b38</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:03:18Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T14:44:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/how-mao-zedong-s-legacy-still-shapes-china-50-years-on/a-79189751--83b4fb70b8d5.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/how-mao-zedong-s-legacy-still-shapes-china-50-years-on/a-79189751?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">China has transformed since Mao Zedong's death 50 years ago. But his legacy endures — from Xi Jinping's rule to a new wave of Mao nostalgia among young Chinese.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Japan's empty temples seek new ways to survive</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9a3d078390c946e32e8bb972111540b88f1f3bd46e322a9be0a7ec2e71ee751e</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:01:56Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T12:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/japan-s-empty-temples-seek-new-ways-to-survive/a-79157964--7eea677a26bf.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/japan-s-empty-temples-seek-new-ways-to-survive/a-79157964?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">With a declining population, shrinking congregations and growing economic pressures, temples are struggling to stay relevant. As more temples are sold or closed, is religion in decline in modern Japan?</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Afghanistan's women: A national soccer team without a home</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f719fc0e9b0b3d9ebe18e91a023f58564bc7940c6038ae1d64c8cc4316ce9561</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:01:56Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T11:03:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/afghanistan-s-women-a-national-soccer-team-without-a-home/a-79139500--7cfb666b9c3a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/afghanistan-s-women-a-national-soccer-team-without-a-home/a-79139500?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The Afghan women's national football team has finally been officially recognized by FIFA. Now the players want to make history both on and off the field.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>India news: Hindu group apologizes over Eiffel Tower row</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c3c388de8249e3d984c30ffe6ecba34ecedf05c976da458f31112c5630e346f2</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:01:56Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T06:38:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/india-news-hindu-group-apologizes-over-eiffel-tower-row/live-79153937--9288b3e28d91.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/india-news-hindu-group-apologizes-over-eiffel-tower-row/live-79153937?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">After the Eiffel Tower was closed amid a staff strike over alleged discrimination, Hindu group BAPS issued an apology, but maintained that no one was denied access. DW has more.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Eiffel Tower employees strike after female staff removed</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cf8180659cb3879e53028a454de344b6e5e32286abfefea6cafee7e08173560e</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:01:56Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T22:23:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/eiffel-tower-employees-strike-after-female-staff-removed/a-79152603--a226c210998d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/eiffel-tower-employees-strike-after-female-staff-removed/a-79152603?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The employees protested against the removal of the site's female workers due to a request by a visiting Hindu group. The tower's operating company apologized for the decision.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>How climate change and food insecurity could fuel future conflicts</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8f04eab3c6bb950309b7f4a82526b088a5f2e1c33e4d5bc70bbb717a6922f373</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:01:56Z</updated><published>2026-09-06T07:47:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/how-climate-change-and-food-insecurity-could-fuel-future-conflicts/a-78871431--8e63f64c4115.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/how-climate-change-and-food-insecurity-could-fuel-future-conflicts/a-78871431?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Climate change, conflict, and trade tensions are putting pressure on the global food system. New research suggests the biggest risk is not food shortages, but whether societies can peacefully adapt to change.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Nepal flood disaster puts climate justice under spotlight</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c320e5bd198413ec1282e6e84288abc20a9195945dd8f724b7ddc04e81b8199a</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:01:56Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T13:59:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nepal-flood-disaster-puts-climate-justice-under-spotlight/a-78998617--8f177ec379f1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nepal-flood-disaster-puts-climate-justice-under-spotlight/a-78998617?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The devastating Himalayan glacial floods have raised broader questions about who should bear the costs and responsibility for the impacts of climate-related disasters.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Why North Korea's women's football team is so good</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:dc0f5c1c58a28e413128286b71ab37c204e56e610e8b1990b6462c80efae3fba</id><updated>2026-09-09T13:01:56Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T10:01:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/why-north-korea-s-women-s-football-team-is-so-good/a-70313505--6b31ccb5ebf0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-north-korea-s-women-s-football-team-is-so-good/a-70313505?maca=en-rss-en-asia-5133-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">North Korea's young footballers enter the U-20 World Cup in Poland as defending champions — the 2024 title was their third. Why is the isolated state so good at the game?</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry></feed>
